Merge adjacent bitfields whose base types differ only in signedness.

Applies to: v1.10.2605.37 (c4d8f4f9). Also applies to main as of 2026-08-28.
Apply after 0002.

HLSL, C++ and DXC's own DXIL back end all merge adjacent bitfields into one
word when the base types have the same width, regardless of signedness. The
SPIR-V back end refuses unless the types match exactly, so a struct written by
the application at one size is read by the shader at another, with nothing to
report it. This engine shares its struct declarations between C++ and HLSL, so
the two sides disagreeing is silent data corruption.

Two of its structs are affected:

  MeshCluster, 32 bytes in C++ and DXIL, 40 in SPIR-V. It has two mixed pairs,
  int32_t m_anchorX : 24 with uint32_t m_numVertices : 8, and the same for Y.

  RenderView, 352 in C++ and DXIL, 360 in SPIR-V. Its three adjacent bitfields
  have three different types - two distinct enums and a uint - and the same
  refusal splits them into three words.

The refusal was not arbitrary. SPIR-V really cannot express a member that is
both signed and unsigned, and OpBitFieldSExtract and OpBitFieldUExtract both
require Base and Result Type to be the *same type*, not merely the same width -
spirv-val rejects `OpBitFieldUExtract %uint %int_base`. So merging needs the
accesses fixed too, and that is most of this patch.

The merged member takes the first field's type. Each field is then reached by
reinterpreting the member into the field's own type and extracting in that
type, so Base and Result Type agree and the choice between the signed and the
unsigned opcode still follows the field. That is what makes an unsigned field
zero-extend out of a signed member:

    %35 = OpBitcast %int %34
    %36 = OpBitFieldSExtract %int %35 %uint_0 %uint_24     ; int sgn : 24
    %40 = OpBitcast %uint %39
    %41 = OpBitFieldUExtract %uint %40 %uint_24 %uint_8    ; uint uns : 8

Writes are the mirror: the value is reinterpreted into the member's type before
OpBitFieldInsert, which has the same same-type requirement.

Four places change.

1. LowerTypeVisitor::populateLayoutInformation decides which fields share a
   member. Same-width integers differing in signedness are now merged.
2. AlignmentSizeCalculator::getAlignmentAndSize computes sizes and offsets from
   the AST independently, and has its own copy of the rule. The two must agree
   or the member list and the offsets describe different structs; leaving this
   one alone produces a struct whose members are merged and whose stride says
   otherwise.
3. SpirvEmitter's bitfield member access names the merged member's type for the
   access chain, and hands the field's type to createLoad separately.
4. SpirvBuilder::createLoad and ::createStore insert the reinterprets.

Effect on this engine: all 46 shader stages compile and pass spirv-val, and
every structured buffer stride now equals the size the engine's own
HLSL_STATIC_ASSERTs demand - MeshCluster included, which it was not before. 32
of the 46 stages are byte-identical to the previous compiler; the 14 that change
are exactly those reading MeshCluster or RenderView, and they change to the
Direct3D layout.

Effect on DXC's own tests: three fail, and all three encode the behaviour this
patch changes rather than catching a fault in it.

  vk.layout.struct.bitfield.hlsl expects `%S6 = OpTypeStruct %uint %int` for
  `struct S6 { uint f2 : 1; int f1 : 1; }`. It is now one member. Compiling the
  same struct with _Static_assert( sizeof( S6 ) == 4 ) on the DXIL back end
  passes, so one member is the Direct3D answer.

  vk.layout.struct.bitfield.assignment.hlsl expects three separate members for
  `struct S3 { uint f1 : 1; int f2 : 1; uint f3 : 1; }` and access chains at
  %int_0, %int_1 and %int_2. All three now live at %int_0, at bit offsets 0, 1
  and 2, with a bitcast on the signed one.

  op.structured-buffer.access.bitfield.hlsl fails for the same reason.

Upstream would want those three updated in a submission. They are deliberately
not touched here, so that the fork's diff against the pinned tag stays confined
to the fix.

